{"parentUuid":"de7e67c5-820d-444b-8088-3d26a1b93db3","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"3b2e7c35-1a45-4dd2-a323-bff2a4559d42","agentId":"ac3048c66f3643aa6","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018B6g6FgwLkUaBQHMwSWCp8","type":"tool_result","content":"395\t| RBSS | unknown |\n396\t| PLGM | unknown |\n397\t\n398\t### Grey A6 (CAN-CH) -- adapter switch: HS\n399\t\n400\t| Module | Confidence |\n401\t|---|---|\n402\t| ABS | unverified |\n403\t| EPS | inferred |\n404\t| ORC | unverified |\n405\t| AFLS | unknown |\n406\t| PAM | unknown |\n407\t| HALF | unknown |\n408\t| TVM | unknown |\n409\t| ESL | unknown |\n410\t"}]},"uuid":"d30981e7-3615-46d8-880b-ccaa6106272d","timestamp":"2026-10-14T18:13:15.275Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\User\\mcp-servers\\docs\\handbook\\07-first-live-session.md","content":"| RBSS | unknown |\n| PLGM | unknown |\n\n### Grey A6 (CAN-CH) -- adapter switch: HS\n\n| Module | Confidence |\n|---|---|\n| ABS | unverified |\n| EPS | inferred |\n| ORC | unverified |\n| AFLS | unknown |\n| PAM | unknown |\n| HALF | unknown |\n| TVM | unknown |\n| ESL | unknown |\n","numLines":16,"startLine":395,"totalLines":412}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"de7e67c5-820d-444b-8088-3d26a1b93db3","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\User\\mcp-servers","sessionId":"7cb02eb5-e299-4baf-b34b-9eb82aebbf17","version":"2.1.282","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"binary-splashing-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"d30981e7-3615-46d8-880b-ccaa6106272d","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac3048c66f3643aa6","attachment":{"type":"total_tokens_reminder","text":"<total_tokens>14728839 tokens left</total_tokens>"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"522b3792-4997-4986-9255-73b222ef3ca0","timestamp":"2026-10-14T18:13:15.308Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\n<total_tokens>14728839 tokens left</total_tokens>\n</system-reminder>"}],"userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\User\\mcp-servers","sessionId":"7cb02eb5-e299-4baf-b34b-9eb82aebbf17","version":"2.1.282","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"binary-splashing-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"522b3792-4997-4986-9255-73b222ef3ca0","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac3048c66f3643aa6","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011Cg2amPR47ihcExZiN48oW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":376,"cache_read_input_tokens":271007,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":376,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"serverClassifierRequest":"2521967a-9848-40db-9b3b-6851b99bda91","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Cg2amNykhkQjvspoST1mB","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"40e5e8c9-46d0-47a4-b4b9-b3c9a8eb8ea7","timestamp":"2026-10-14T18:13:18.523Z","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":null,"userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\User\\mcp-servers","sessionId":"7cb02eb5-e299-4baf-b34b-9eb82aebbf17","version":"2.1.282","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"binary-splashing-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"40e5e8c9-46d0-47a4-b4b9-b3c9a8eb8ea7","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac3048c66f3643aa6","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011Cg2amPR47ihcExZiN48oW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Ezz1ZbCWzxaGPZ9jcjjhKi","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"C:\\Users\\User\\mcp-servers\\docs\\handbook\\07-first-live-session.md","old_string":"<!-- cable-reference:end -->","new_string":"<!-- cable-reference:end -->\n\n## 9. Learning module parameters from MES (a second adapter on CAN-C)\n\nSection 7 learns an *unscaled* broadcast signal from a bypass cable by\nwatching what changed while you held a physical action. This is different:\nit learns a *scaled* module DID -- a named channel with a real unit, not\n\"learned, unscaled\" -- by overhearing MultiEcuScan's own UDS traffic on\nCAN-C while MES graphs and records a parameter you already trust MES to\ndecode correctly. Nothing here replaces or edits a catalogue DID\n(`cuore.live.did_catalog`); every entry it produces is stamped confidence\n`SINGLE-SOURCE (learned)` and kept in its own store.\n\n### Hardware\n\n- **A Y-splitter on the OBD-II port**, wired to CAN-C (pins 6/14) so two\n  adapters can read the same bus at once.\n- **A second adapter**, on its *own* COM port, different from MES's own\n  interface port: another STN/vLinker, or a USB-CAN interface. This is the\n  one requirement the software cannot work around -- `mes_learn_start`\n  refuses outright, naming exactly this, if only one adapter is detected at\n  all, or if the port given is the same one MES is configured to use\n  (`Interface 0 Port` in MES's own settings).\n- MultiEcuScan, running its normal live view against its own adapter on its\n  own port, recording a CSV (MES's graph subsystem, up to 4 graphs of 10\n  parameters each -- see `docs/format/CSV_LOG_FORMAT.md`).\n\n### Procedure\n\n1. Fit the splitter and the second adapter; note its COM port (`list_ports()`\n   marks MES's own interface so you can tell them apart).\n2. `mes_learn_start(port=\"COM<n>\")` (or the \"Second adapter's port\" field at\n   `/v/{vin}/learn-mes`). This opens the second adapter **passively**\n   (`STCMM 0`, receive-only, same as every other passive listen in this\n   project -- see `cuore.live.capture.listen`): it never acknowledges a\n   frame and never transmits a byte of its own onto the bus.\n3. In MES, start the live view/graph for the parameters you want decoded,\n   and record a CSV. Let it run long enough that each parameter you care\n   about actually moves through at least 20 distinct values -- a channel\n   that never leaves one value, or only ever takes a handful of discrete\n   states (a 9-position gear indicator, say), cannot be fitted with\n   confidence and is reported as such, not guessed.\n4. `mes_learn_stop(csv=\"<the CSV MES just wrote>\")` (bare filename, or a\n   full path). This reassembles ISO-TP, pairs every UDS request/response\n   the second adapter overheard (`cuore.live.learn.uds_transactions` --\n   29-bit `18DAxxF1`/`18DAF1xx` and the 11-bit pair alike), groups by\n   `(module, DID)`, and fits each named CSV column against every\n   `(module, DID, byte offset, width, signedness)` seen, by ordinary least\n   squares over the whole session.\n5. `mes_learn_candidates()` (or the candidates table at `/v/{vin}/\n   learn-mes`) lists the result: a confident fit (R² >= 0.98 over >= 20\n   distinct raw values) per column, with its module, DID, field, scale,\n   offset and R²; anything short of that bar is listed `\"no confident\n   match\"` rather than a guessed scale.\n6. Review each candidate against what MES labelled that column, then\n   **Accept** (saves it through `cuore.live.learned.accept_mes` with source\n   `\"learned from MES <csv name> <date>, fit R² <value>\"` -- immediately\n   usable by the Live board as a named, scaled channel, the same hand-off\n   `cuore.live.ui_store.load_custom_channels` already uses for custom\n   channels) or **Reject** (kept observed-but-unscaled, never guessed, same\n   posture as rejecting a Section 7 broadcast candidate).\n\nThe MCP tools are `mes_learn_start(port, vin=\"\")`, `mes_learn_stop(csv=\"\")`,\n`mes_learn_status()`, `mes_learn_candidates()` and `mes_learn_accept\n(candidate_id, vin, name)`; rejecting a candidate is a page/API action\n(`POST /api/mes-learn/candidates/{id}/reject`), not an MCP tool, same split\nas Section 7's learn-accept."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":376,"cache_read_input_tokens":271007,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":376,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01Ezz1ZbCWzxaGPZ9jcjjhKi":{"file_path":"C:\\Users\\User\\mcp-servers\\docs\\handbook\\07-first-live-session.md","old_string":"<!-- cable-reference:end -->","new_string":"<!-- cable-reference:end -->\n\n## 9. Learning module parameters from MES (a second adapter on CAN-C)\n\nSection 7 learns an *unscaled* broadcast signal from a bypass cable by\nwatching what changed while you held a physical action. This is different:\nit learns a *scaled* module DID -- a named channel with a real unit, not\n\"learned, unscaled\" -- by overhearing MultiEcuScan's own UDS traffic on\nCAN-C while MES graphs and records a parameter you already trust MES to\ndecode correctly. Nothing here replaces or edits a catalogue DID\n(`cuore.live.did_catalog`); every entry it produces is stamped confidence\n`SINGLE-SOURCE (learned)` and kept in its own store.\n\n### Hardware\n\n- **A Y-splitter on the OBD-II port**, wired to CAN-C (pins 6/14) so two\n  adapters can read the same bus at once.\n- **A second adapter**, on its *own* COM port, different from MES's own\n  interface port: another STN/vLinker, or a USB-CAN interface. This is the\n  one requirement the software cannot work around -- `mes_learn_start`\n  refuses outright, naming exactly this, if only one adapter is detected at\n  all, or if the port given is the same one MES is configured to use\n  (`Interface 0 Port` in MES's own settings).\n- MultiEcuScan, running its normal live view against its own adapter on its\n  own port, recording a CSV (MES's graph subsystem, up to 4 graphs of 10\n  parameters each -- see `docs/format/CSV_LOG_FORMAT.md`).\n\n### Procedure\n\n1. Fit the splitter and the second adapter; note its COM port (`list_ports()`\n   marks MES's own interface so you can tell them apart).\n2. `mes_learn_start(port=\"COM<n>\")` (or the \"Second adapter's port\" field at\n   `/v/{vin}/learn-mes`). This opens the second adapter **passively**\n   (`STCMM 0`, receive-only, same as every other passive listen in this\n   project -- see `cuore.live.capture.listen`): it never acknowledges a\n   frame and never transmits a byte of its own onto the bus.\n3. In MES, start the live view/graph for the parameters you want decoded,\n   and record a CSV. Let it run long enough that each parameter you care\n   about actually moves through at least 20 distinct values -- a channel\n   that never leaves one value, or only ever takes a handful of discrete\n   states (a 9-position gear indicator, say), cannot be fitted with\n   confidence and is reported as such, not guessed.\n4. `mes_learn_stop(csv=\"<the CSV MES just wrote>\")` (bare filename, or a\n   full path). This reassembles ISO-TP, pairs every UDS request/response\n   the second adapter overheard (`cuore.live.learn.uds_transactions` --\n   29-bit `18DAxxF1`/`18DAF1xx` and the 11-bit pair alike), groups by\n   `(module, DID)`, and fits each named CSV column against every\n   `(module, DID, byte offset, width, signedness)` seen, by ordinary least\n   squares over the whole session.\n5. `mes_learn_candidates()` (or the candidates table at `/v/{vin}/\n   learn-mes`) lists the result: a confident fit (R\\u00b2 >= 0.98 over >= 20\n   distinct raw values) per column, with its module, DID, field, scale,\n   offset and R\\u00b2; anything short of that bar is listed `\"no confident\n   match\"` rather than a guessed scale.\n6. Review each candidate against what MES labelled that column, then\n   **Accept** (saves it through `cuore.live.learned.accept_mes` with source\n   `\"learned from MES <csv name> <date>, fit R\\u00b2 <value>\"` -- immediately\n   usable by the Live board as a named, scaled channel, the same hand-off\n   `cuore.live.ui_store.load_custom_channels` already uses for custom\n   channels) or **Reject** (kept observed-but-unscaled, never guessed, same\n   posture as rejecting a Section 7 broadcast candidate).\n\nThe MCP tools are `mes_learn_start(port, vin=\"\")`, `mes_learn_stop(csv=\"\")`,\n`mes_learn_status()`, `mes_learn_candidates()` and `mes_learn_accept\n(candidate_id, vin, name)`; rejecting a candidate is a page/API action\n(`POST /api/mes-learn/candidates/{id}/reject`), not an MCP tool, same split\nas Section 7's learn-accept."}},"serverClassifierRequest":"2521967a-9848-40db-9b3b-6851b99bda91","apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cg2amNykhkQjvspoST1mB","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"6838320d-2931-4f08-a1f9-faeb34898b75","timestamp":"2026-10-14T18:13:37.078Z","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":null,"userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\User\\mcp-servers","sessionId":"7cb02eb5-e299-4baf-b34b-9eb82aebbf17","version":"2.1.282","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"binary-splashing-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"6838320d-2931-4f08-a1f9-faeb34898b75","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"3b2e7c35-1a45-4dd2-a323-bff2a4559d42","agentId":"ac3048c66f3643aa6","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ezz1ZbCWzxaGPZ9jcjjhKi","type":"tool_result","content":"The file C:\\Users\\User\\mcp-servers\\docs\\handbook\\07-first-live-session.md has been updated successfully."}]},"uuid":"f60c9813-9cc7-491d-90e7-5ca95f87fd6e","timestamp":"2026-10-14T18:13:37.519Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\User\\mcp-servers\\docs\\handbook\\07-first-live-session.md","oldString":"<!-- cable-reference:end -->","newString":"<!-- cable-reference:end -->\n\n## 9. Learning module parameters from MES (a second adapter on CAN-C)\n\nSection 7 learns an *unscaled* broadcast signal from a bypass cable by\nwatching what changed while you held a physical action. This is different:\nit learns a *scaled* module DID -- a named channel with a real unit, not\n\"learned, unscaled\" -- by overhearing MultiEcuScan's own UDS traffic on\nCAN-C while MES graphs and records a parameter you already trust MES to\ndecode correctly. Nothing here replaces or edits a catalogue DID\n(`cuore.live.did_catalog`); every entry it produces is stamped confidence\n`SINGLE-SOURCE (learned)` and kept in its own store.\n\n### Hardware\n\n- **A Y-splitter on the OBD-II port**, wired to CAN-C (pins 6/14) so two\n  adapters can read the same bus at once.\n- **A second adapter**, on its *own* COM port, different from MES's own\n  interface port: another STN/vLinker, or a USB-CAN interface. This is the\n  one requirement the software cannot work around -- `mes_learn_start`\n  refuses outright, naming exactly this, if only one adapter is detected at\n  all, or if the port given is the same one MES is configured to use\n  (`Interface 0 Port` in MES's own settings).\n- MultiEcuScan, running its normal live view against its own adapter on its\n  own port, recording a CSV (MES's graph subsystem, up to 4 graphs of 10\n  parameters each -- see `docs/format/CSV_LOG_FORMAT.md`).\n\n### Procedure\n\n1. Fit the splitter and the second adapter; note its COM port (`list_ports()`\n   marks MES's own interface so you can tell them apart).\n2. `mes_learn_start(port=\"COM<n>\")` (or the \"Second adapter's port\" field at\n   `/v/{vin}/learn-mes`). This opens the second adapter **passively**\n   (`STCMM 0`, receive-only, same as every other passive listen in this\n   project -- see `cuore.live.capture.listen`): it never acknowledges a\n   frame and never transmits a byte of its own onto the bus.\n3. In MES, start the live view/graph for the parameters you want decoded,\n   and record a CSV. Let it run long enough that each parameter you care\n   about actually moves through at least 20 distinct values -- a channel\n   that never leaves one value, or only ever takes a handful of discrete\n   states (a 9-position gear indicator, say), cannot be fitted with\n   confidence and is reported as such, not guessed.\n4. `mes_learn_stop(csv=\"<the CSV MES just wrote>\")` (bare filename, or a\n   full path). This reassembles ISO-TP, pairs every UDS request/response\n   the second adapter overheard (`cuore.live.learn.uds_transactions` --\n   29-bit `18DAxxF1`/`18DAF1xx` and the 11-bit pair alike), groups by\n   `(module, DID)`, and fits each named CSV column against every\n   `(module, DID, byte offset, width, signedness)` seen, by ordinary least\n   squares over the whole session.\n5. `mes_learn_candidates()` (or the candidates table at `/v/{vin}/\n   learn-mes`) lists the result: a confident fit (R² >= 0.98 over >= 20\n   distinct raw values) per column, with its module, DID, field, scale,\n   offset and R²; anything short of that bar is listed `\"no confident\n   match\"` rather than a guessed scale.\n6. Review each candidate against what MES labelled that column, then\n   **Accept** (saves it through `cuore.live.learned.accept_mes` with source\n   `\"learned from MES <csv name> <date>, fit R² <value>\"` -- immediately\n   usable by the Live board as a named, scaled channel, the same hand-off\n   `cuore.live.ui_store.load_custom_channels` already uses for custom\n   channels) or **Reject** (kept observed-but-unscaled, never guessed, same\n   posture as rejecting a Section 7 broadcast candidate).\n\nThe MCP tools are `mes_learn_start(port, vin=\"\")`, `mes_learn_stop(csv=\"\")`,\n`mes_learn_status()`, `mes_learn_candidates()` and `mes_learn_accept\n(candidate_id, vin, name)`; rejecting a candidate is a page/API action\n(`POST /api/mes-learn/candidates/{id}/reject`), not an MCP tool, same split\nas Section 7's learn-accept.","originalFile":null,"structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":409,"oldLines":3,"newStart":409,"newLines":69,"lines":[" | ESL | unknown |"," "," <!-- cable-reference:end -->","+","+## 9. Learning module parameters from MES (a second adapter on CAN-C)","+","+Section 7 learns an *unscaled* broadcast signal from a bypass cable by","+watching what changed while you held a physical action. This is different:","+it learns a *scaled* module DID -- a named channel with a real unit, not","+\"learned, unscaled\" -- by overhearing MultiEcuScan's own UDS traffic on","+CAN-C while MES graphs and records a parameter you already trust MES to","+decode correctly. Nothing here replaces or edits a catalogue DID","+(`cuore.live.did_catalog`); every entry it produces is stamped confidence","+`SINGLE-SOURCE (learned)` and kept in its own store.","+","+### Hardware","+","+- **A Y-splitter on the OBD-II port**, wired to CAN-C (pins 6/14) so two","+  adapters can read the same bus at once.","+- **A second adapter**, on its *own* COM port, different from MES's own","+  interface port: another STN/vLinker, or a USB-CAN interface. This is the","+  one requirement the software cannot work around -- `mes_learn_start`","+  refuses outright, naming exactly this, if only one adapter is detected at","+  all, or if the port given is the same one MES is configured to use","+  (`Interface 0 Port` in MES's own settings).","+- MultiEcuScan, running its normal live view against its own adapter on its","+  own port, recording a CSV (MES's graph subsystem, up to 4 graphs of 10","+  parameters each -- see `docs/format/CSV_LOG_FORMAT.md`).","+","+### Procedure","+","+1. Fit the splitter and the second adapter; note its COM port (`list_ports()`","+   marks MES's own interface so you can tell them apart).","+2. `mes_learn_start(port=\"COM<n>\")` (or the \"Second adapter's port\" field at","+   `/v/{vin}/learn-mes`). This opens the second adapter **passively**","+   (`STCMM 0`, receive-only, same as every other passive listen in this","+   project -- see `cuore.live.capture.listen`): it never acknowledges a","+   frame and never transmits a byte of its own onto the bus.","+3. In MES, start the live view/graph for the parameters you want decoded,","+   and record a CSV. Let it run long enough that each parameter you care","+   about actually moves through at least 20 distinct values -- a channel","+   that never leaves one value, or only ever takes a handful of discrete","+   states (a 9-position gear indicator, say), cannot be fitted with","+   confidence and is reported as such, not guessed.","+4. `mes_learn_stop(csv=\"<the CSV MES just wrote>\")` (bare filename, or a","+   full path). This reassembles ISO-TP, pairs every UDS request/response","+   the second adapter overheard (`cuore.live.learn.uds_transactions` --","+   29-bit `18DAxxF1`/`18DAF1xx` and the 11-bit pair alike), groups by","+   `(module, DID)`, and fits each named CSV column against every","+   `(module, DID, byte offset, width, signedness)` seen, by ordinary least","+   squares over the whole session.","+5. `mes_learn_candidates()` (or the candidates table at `/v/{vin}/","+   learn-mes`) lists the result: a confident fit (R² >= 0.98 over >= 20","+   distinct raw values) per column, with its module, DID, field, scale,","+   offset and R²; anything short of that bar is listed `\"no confident","+   match\"` rather than a guessed scale.","+6. Review each candidate against what MES labelled that column, then","+   **Accept** (saves it through `cuore.live.learned.accept_mes` with source","+   `\"learned from MES <csv name> <date>, fit R² <value>\"` -- immediately","+   usable by the Live board as a named, scaled channel, the same hand-off","+   `cuore.live.ui_store.load_custom_channels` already uses for custom","+   channels) or **Reject** (kept observed-but-unscaled, never guessed, same","+   posture as rejecting a Section 7 broadcast candidate).","+","+The MCP tools are `mes_learn_start(port, vin=\"\")`, `mes_learn_stop(csv=\"\")`,","+`mes_learn_status()`, `mes_learn_candidates()` and `mes_learn_accept","+(candidate_id, vin, name)`; rejecting a candidate is a page/API action","+(`POST /api/mes-learn/candidates/{id}/reject`), not an MCP tool, same split","+as Section 7's learn-accept."]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false,"contentNotInModelContext":true},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"2521967a-9848-40db-9b3b-6851b99bda91","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/c/Users/User/mcp-servers","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/c/Users/User/mcp-servers","platform":"windows"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"6838320d-2931-4f08-a1f9-faeb34898b75","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\User\\mcp-servers","sessionId":"7cb02eb5-e299-4baf-b34b-9eb82aebbf17","version":"2.1.282","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"binary-splashing-comet"}
